Brute Force
-----------
Idea:
- Store all elements in a vector.
- For insertion, check whether the element already exists.
- For deletion, search for the element and erase it.
- Pick a random index for getRandom().

Algorithm:
1. For insert():
   - Traverse the vector.
   - If value exists, return false.
   - Otherwise, push_back().
2. For remove():
   - Traverse the vector.
   - If found, erase it.
3. For getRandom():
   - Return nums[rand() % nums.size()].

Time Complexity:
- insert: O(n)
- remove: O(n)
- getRandom: O(1)

Space Complexity: O(n)

```cpp
class RandomizedSet {
    vector<int> nums;

public:
    RandomizedSet() {}

    bool insert(int val) {
        for (int x : nums)
            if (x == val)
                return false;

        nums.push_back(val);
        return true;
    }

    bool remove(int val) {
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {
            if (nums[i] == val) {
                nums.erase(nums.begin() + i);
                return true;
            }
        }

        return false;
    }

    int getRandom() {
        return nums[rand() % nums.size()];
    }
};
```

Better Solution
---------------
No intermediate improvement exists. We can directly optimize from brute force to the optimal approach.

Optimal Solution
----------------
Idea:
- Use:
  - A vector to store elements.
  - A hash map to store each element's index in the vector.
- For deletion:
  - Swap the element to delete with the last element.
  - Update the last element's index in the hash map.
  - Remove the last element from the vector.
  - Delete the removed value from the hash map.
- This avoids the O(n) shifting caused by vector::erase().

Algorithm:
1. insert(val):
   - If val already exists, return false.
   - Store its index in the map.
   - Push it into the vector.
2. remove(val):
   - If val doesn't exist, return false.
   - Find its index.
   - Swap it with the last element.
   - Update the last element's index.
   - Pop the last element.
   - Remove val from the map.
3. getRandom():
   - Return nums[rand() % nums.size()].

Time Complexity:
- insert: O(1)
- remove: O(1)
- getRandom: O(1)

Space Complexity: O(n)

```cpp
class RandomizedSet {
    vector<int> nums;
    unordered_map<int, int> index;

public:
    RandomizedSet() {}

    bool insert(int val) {
        if (index.count(val))
            return false;

        nums.push_back(val);
        index[val] = nums.size() - 1;
        return true;
    }

    bool remove(int val) {
        if (!index.count(val))
            return false;

        int idx = index[val];
        int last = nums.back();

        nums[idx] = last;
        index[last] = idx;

        nums.pop_back();
        index.erase(val);

        return true;
    }

    int getRandom() {
        return nums[rand() % nums.size()];
    }
};
```

Interview Notes
---------------
- A vector provides O(1) random access but O(n) deletion because elements must be shifted.
- A hash map provides O(1) lookup but cannot return a random element efficiently.
- Combining both data structures gives O(1) average time for all operations.
- Why swap with the last element?
  - Removing the last element from a vector is O(1).
  - Swapping avoids shifting every element after the deleted one.
- Example:
  - nums = [10, 20, 30, 40]
  - Remove 20.
  - Swap with 40 → [10, 40, 30, 20]
  - Pop last → [10, 40, 30]
  - Update index of 40 in the map.
- Edge Cases:
  - Insert duplicate value.
  - Remove a non-existing value.
  - Remove the last element.
  - Remove the only element.
- Follow-up:
  - Why not use `unordered_set`?
    - It supports O(1) insert/remove but does not support O(1) random access.
  - Why not call `vector.erase()`?
    - `erase()` shifts all following elements, making deletion O(n).
- This is one of the classic interview questions testing the combination of multiple data structures to achieve constant-time operations.
